sections.body:
The modern (Spring Security 6+) way to declare which URLs require what level of access is a `@Bean` method returning a `SecurityFilterChain`, configured through the `HttpSecurity` object it's handed:

```java
@Configuration
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/public/**").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
        );
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

This registers a real bean, exactly like the `@Bean` methods covered in the Core Concepts module -- there's nothing exotic about the mechanism itself, just a fluent API that ultimately configures which filters get added to the chain and how they're parameterized. `authorizeHttpRequests` rules are evaluated **in the order they're declared, and the first matching rule wins** -- Spring Security does not collect every applicable rule and pick 'the most specific one' automatically. This makes rule *ordering* a real, load-bearing part of the configuration's correctness, not just a stylistic choice: a broad rule declared before a narrower, more specific one can silently make that narrower rule unreachable, because the broad rule already claimed the match first. The safe, idiomatic pattern is therefore to order rules from most specific to least specific, ending with a catch-all `anyRequest()` rule last.

`HttpSecurity` rules govern access at the URL level -- at the *entry point* into the application. `@PreAuthorize`, applied directly to a method (commonly a `@Service` method), governs access at the level of that *specific business operation*, evaluated as a SpEL (Spring Expression Language) expression against the current `Authentication` immediately before the method is allowed to execute:

```java
@Service
public class ReportService {

    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public List<AuditEntry> getAuditLog() {
        // only reachable if the current Authentication has ROLE_ADMIN
        return auditRepository.findAll();
    }
}
```

The granularity difference is the whole point of having both tools available. A URL-based rule is excellent for securing an entire endpoint -- 'nobody without `ROLE_ADMIN` can hit any `/api/admin/**` path at all' -- but it only protects requests that actually arrive *through that URL*. If the exact same business method is also invoked from somewhere else entirely -- a scheduled batch job, an internal call from another service, a message-queue listener -- a URL-based rule provides zero protection for those call paths, because there's no HTTP request, and therefore no URL, involved at all. `@PreAuthorize` on the method itself closes that gap: the check travels with the *operation*, not with any particular route into it, so it's enforced identically no matter what actually calls that method.

`@PreAuthorize` does absolutely nothing on its own -- it is inert metadata until a separate, easy-to-forget annotation, `@EnableMethodSecurity`, is present on some `@Configuration` class in the application:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {
    // ... SecurityFilterChain bean, etc.
}
```

Here's why it's required, mechanistically: Spring only wraps a bean in a security-checking AOP proxy if it's specifically told to look for `@PreAuthorize` (and related annotations like `@PostAuthorize`, `@PreFilter`, `@PostFilter`) and generate that proxying behavior; `@EnableMethodSecurity` is that instruction. Without it, `@PreAuthorize` is just an annotation sitting on a method that nothing ever reads or acts on -- and critically, **there is no error, no warning, and no startup failure** when it's missing. The method simply runs completely unguarded for every caller, regardless of their authorities, which makes this one of the more dangerous configuration gaps to overlook: the code looks correctly secured at a glance (the annotation is right there, clearly readable), but nothing is actually enforcing it. It's genuinely worth writing an integration test that specifically asserts an unauthorized caller IS rejected, rather than trusting the annotation's mere presence.

One more edge case worth knowing about, even briefly: method security is implemented via an AOP proxy wrapped around the bean, and a proxy can only intercept calls that arrive *from outside* the bean, through that proxy. A method calling another `@PreAuthorize`-annotated method *on itself*, from within the same class (self-invocation), bypasses the proxy entirely and therefore bypasses the security check -- a well-known, general limitation of proxy-based Spring AOP, not something specific to security, but one with real security consequences if you're not aware of it.

